export const N6={title:'Maternal, newborn and pediatric nursing',hook:'In the first hour of life, a newborn receives an Apgar score, a vitamin K injection and antibiotic ointment in both eyes. Each step prevents a specific harm. Caring for families through pregnancy, birth and childhood requires knowledge of normal changes, warning signs and how children differ from adults.',objectives:['Describe prenatal care, the stages of labor and postpartum assessment.','Assess the newborn with the Apgar score and provide routine newborn care.','Perform neonatal eye prophylaxis following HOSA Skill VII.','Adapt nursing care to children’s growth, development and vital signs.'],sections:[
['Pregnancy and prenatal care',[
'Pregnancy lasts about **40 weeks** from the first day of the last menstrual period and is divided into three **trimesters**. Prenatal care monitors maternal blood pressure, weight, urine, fetal growth and heart rate, and screens for conditions such as **gestational diabetes** (often tested at 24–28 weeks) and **preeclampsia** (new high blood pressure after 20 weeks with protein in the urine or organ involvement, with headache, visual changes and swelling as warning signs). Folic acid before and during early pregnancy reduces neural tube defects. Rh-negative mothers receive Rh immune globulin to prevent sensitization. **Gravida** is the number of pregnancies; **para** the number of births after 20 weeks.']],
['Labor, birth and postpartum',[
'Labor has four **stages**. The **first stage** runs from the onset of regular contractions to **full cervical dilation (10 cm)** and includes latent, active and transition phases. The **second stage** is from full dilation to the **birth of the baby**. The **third stage** is delivery of the **placenta**. The **fourth stage** is the first **1–4 hours of recovery**, when the risk of **postpartum hemorrhage** is highest. Postpartum nurses assess the **fundus** (it should be firm and midline; a boggy fundus is massaged to prevent hemorrhage), **lochia** (vaginal discharge progressing from red **rubra** to pink **serosa** to white **alba**), the perineum, bladder, breasts, pain, bonding and mood, watching for postpartum depression.',
order('Stages of labor',['First stage: regular contractions to full dilation (10 cm)','Second stage: full dilation to birth of the baby','Third stage: delivery of the placenta','Fourth stage: first hours of recovery'])]],
['Newborn assessment and routine care',[
'The **Apgar score** is assessed at **1 and 5 minutes** after birth. Five signs are each scored 0–2: **A**ppearance (color), **P**ulse (heart rate), **G**rimace (reflex irritability), **A**ctivity (muscle tone) and **R**espiration. A score of **7–10** is reassuring; lower scores prompt intervention and repeat scoring. Newborns lose heat quickly, so they are dried and placed **skin-to-skin** or under a warmer. Routine care includes **vitamin K** intramuscularly (newborns have low vitamin K, and the injection prevents **vitamin K deficiency bleeding**), **eye prophylaxis** with **erythromycin ophthalmic ointment** to prevent **gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum**, the first **hepatitis B vaccine**, identification bands, newborn screening blood tests, a hearing screen and monitoring for **jaundice**. Normal newborn heart rate is about 110–160 beats per minute and respirations about 30–60 per minute.',
match('Match the newborn care to its purpose',[['Vitamin K injection','Prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding'],['Erythromycin eye ointment','Prevents gonococcal eye infection'],['Apgar score at 1 and 5 minutes','Evaluates transition to life outside the womb'],['Skin-to-skin contact','Maintains temperature and supports bonding'],['Newborn screening heel stick','Detects treatable metabolic and genetic disorders'],['Hepatitis B vaccine','Begins protection against hepatitis B']])]],
['Neonatal eye prophylaxis (HOSA Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII, the competitor performs hand hygiene, puts on **nonlatex gloves** and assembles supplies. They **clean over the closed eyelids** and surrounding area with **sterile cotton balls moistened with sterile water** and dry with soft sterile gauze. Stabilizing the baby’s head and **shading the eyes** from overhead light, they **gently separate the lids** of one eye by pressing on the upper and lower lids and place a thin **1–2 cm line of erythromycin ophthalmic ointment** along the **conjunctival sac**, moving from the **inner canthus to the outer canthus**, without touching the eyelid or eyeball with the tube. The used ointment is discarded, and the steps are repeated for the other eye. The competitor verbalizes closing the eye and counting to 5, manipulates the eyelid carefully, and after **1 minute wipes excess ointment** from the eyelid and skin with sterile water, then repeats for the other eye. The skill ends with communication, glove removal, hand hygiene, standard precautions and documentation.',
order('Neonatal eye prophylaxis (HOSA Skill VII)',['Hand hygiene and nonlatex gloves','Clean closed eyelids with sterile water–moistened cotton and dry','Stabilize the head and shade the eyes','Separate the lids of one eye','Apply a 1–2 cm ribbon of erythromycin inner to outer canthus without touching the eye','Repeat for the other eye','Close each eye briefly; after 1 minute wipe excess ointment','Remove gloves, hand hygiene and document'])]],
['Caring for children',[
'Children are not small adults. Vital signs vary with age: a toddler’s heart rate (roughly 90–150) and respiratory rate are higher than an adult’s, and blood pressure is lower. Medication doses are calculated by **weight (mg/kg)**, and the HOSA math convention is to round pediatric doses **down**. Growth is tracked on growth charts; development is checked against **milestones** such as social smiling around 2 months, sitting without support around 6 months, walking around 12 months and two-word phrases around 24 months. Developmental theory guides communication: toddlers fear separation, preschoolers have vivid fears about body harm, school-age children like explanations and participation and adolescents value privacy and peer acceptance.',
'Safety teaching includes **back-to-sleep** positioning on a firm, flat surface to reduce sudden unexpected infant death, rear-facing car seats for young children, poison prevention, water safety and the routine **immunization schedule**. Signs of dehydration in infants include fewer wet diapers, a sunken fontanel and no tears. Nurses are **mandated reporters** of suspected child abuse.',
sort('Typical age for the milestone',['About 2 months','About 6 months','About 12 months','About 24 months'],[['Social smile',0],['Sits without support',1],['Walks a few steps',2],['Says two-word phrases',3],['Holds head up during tummy time',0],['First words like “mama”',2]])]],
['Applying the concepts',[
kase('A firm or boggy fundus?',{a:{text:'Thirty minutes after delivery, a mother’s fundus is soft (boggy) and above the umbilicus, and lochia is heavy with clots. What should the nurse do first?',choices:[['Massage the fundus while supporting the lower uterus','b','Correct. Massage stimulates contraction and reduces bleeding.',true],['Encourage her to rest and recheck in 2 hours','b','A boggy fundus with heavy bleeding needs immediate action.',false]]},b:{text:'The fundus becomes firm but deviates to the right. What does this suggest?',choices:[['A full bladder displacing the uterus; help her void','c','Correct. A full bladder prevents contraction.',true],['Normal involution needing no action','c','Deviation suggests bladder distension.',false]]},c:{text:'After voiding, the fundus is firm and midline, bleeding decreases and the nurse continues frequent assessments during the fourth stage.',end:true}})]],
["High-risk pregnancy and pediatric emergencies",["Nurses recognize warning signs in pregnancy and childhood. **Preeclampsia** can progress to **eclampsia** (seizures) and **HELLP syndrome** (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets); **magnesium sulfate** prevents seizures, and nurses watch for magnesium toxicity—loss of deep tendon reflexes, respirations below 12 and decreased urine output—with **calcium gluconate** as the antidote. **Placenta previa** (placenta over the cervix) causes **painless bright red bleeding**, so vaginal exams are avoided, whereas **placental abruption** (early separation) causes **painful bleeding with a rigid abdomen**. **Gestational diabetes** raises the risk of large babies and newborn hypoglycemia. **Preterm labor** before 37 weeks may be treated to delay birth and with corticosteroids to mature fetal lungs.","In children, **dehydration** from vomiting and diarrhea develops quickly; nurses track weight, wet diapers, mucous membranes and fontanels and encourage **oral rehydration solution**. **Respiratory distress** shows as nasal flaring, **retractions**, grunting and a rising respiratory rate, often from **bronchiolitis** (RSV) or asthma. **Febrile seizures** occur in young children with a rapid rise in temperature. Signs of possible **child maltreatment** include injuries inconsistent with the history, patterned bruises and delays in seeking care; nurses document objectively and report as mandated. Accidental **poisoning** prevention and safe medication storage are taught to every family.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Placenta previa or placental abruption?","buckets":["Placenta previa","Placental abruption"],"items":[["Painless bright red bleeding",0],["Avoid vaginal examinations",0],["Painful bleeding with a rigid abdomen",1],["Often linked to trauma or hypertension",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Labor stages: dilation to 10 cm, birth, placenta, recovery; check that the fundus is firm and midline and track lochia rubra, serosa, alba.','Apgar at 1 and 5 minutes; newborns receive vitamin K, erythromycin eye ointment and hepatitis B vaccine.','Skill VII: clean lids, shade eyes, 1–2 cm ribbon inner to outer canthus without touching, wipe excess after 1 minute; children need weight-based dosing and age-appropriate care.'],terms:[['Apgar score','Newborn assessment of appearance, pulse, grimace, activity and respiration.'],['Lochia','Postpartum vaginal discharge.'],['Fundus','Top of the uterus, assessed after birth for firmness and position.']],mcqs:[q('Which ointment is commonly used for neonatal eye prophylaxis?','Erythromycin ophthalmic ointment','Hydrocortisone cream','Petroleum jelly','Silver sulfadiazine','It prevents gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum.'),q('In Skill VII, ointment is applied:','From the inner canthus to the outer canthus','From the outer canthus to the inner canthus','On the eyelid skin only','Directly onto the cornea with the tube touching','The tube must not touch the eye.'),q('Why do newborns receive vitamin K?','To prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding','To prevent jaundice','To boost immunity','To improve eyesight','Newborns have low vitamin K.'),q('When is the Apgar score assessed?','1 and 5 minutes after birth','At 24 hours','Only at discharge','Before birth','Additional scores follow if low.'),q('The second stage of labor ends with:','Birth of the baby','Full cervical dilation','Delivery of the placenta','Discharge','The third stage delivers the placenta.'),q('A boggy fundus after delivery should be:','Massaged while supporting the lower uterus','Ignored','Treated with ice only','Pushed downward forcefully','Massage promotes contraction.'),q('Pediatric doses are usually calculated by:','Weight in mg/kg','Age alone','Height only','Adult dose divided by two','HOSA rounds pediatric doses down.'),q('Lochia progresses in which order?','Rubra, serosa, alba','Alba, serosa, rubra','Serosa, rubra, alba','Rubra, alba, serosa','Red to pink to white.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the care of a newborn in the first hour after birth, including Apgar scoring, routine medications and the eye prophylaxis procedure from HOSA Skill VII, and explain the purpose of each step.',rubric:[['Apgar scoring',['apgar','1 and 5','appearance','pulse','respiration','7']],['Routine care and purposes',['vitamin k','bleeding','hepatitis b','skin-to-skin','temperature']],['Skill VII technique',['clean','sterile water','shade','1-2 cm','1–2 cm','inner','outer','not touch']],['Purpose of eye prophylaxis and documentation',['gonococcal','ophthalmia','infection','document']]],model:'At 1 and 5 minutes after birth, the nurse scores appearance, pulse, grimace, activity and respiration from 0 to 2 each; 7–10 is reassuring. The newborn is dried and placed skin-to-skin to prevent heat loss and support bonding, receives intramuscular vitamin K to prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding and often the first hepatitis B vaccine, and is banded for identification. For eye prophylaxis, the nurse performs hand hygiene and puts on nonlatex gloves, cleans the closed eyelids with sterile water–moistened cotton balls and dries them, stabilizes the head and shades the eyes, separates the lids and applies a thin 1–2 cm ribbon of erythromycin ointment along the conjunctival sac from the inner to the outer canthus without touching the eye, repeats for the other eye, then wipes away excess after about one minute. This prevents gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum, an eye infection that can cause blindness, and the nurse documents the procedure.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_OPHTH,AAP_VITK,CDC_MILESTONES]};

export const N7={title:'Adults, older adults, mental health and end-of-life care',hook:'One nurse’s day might include helping an 80-year-old get up safely after a fall, calming a patient with acute anxiety, visiting a home-care client managing oxygen and caring for a patient’s body after death with dignity. Clinical care across the life span means adapting to age, setting and need—including the last care a nurse provides.',objectives:['Describe age-related changes and common risks in older adults, including delirium and dementia.','Apply therapeutic communication and safety in mental health nursing.','Compare home care, extended care, rehabilitation, palliative care and hospice.','Perform postmortem care following HOSA Skill V.'],sections:[
['Adulthood and aging',[
'Adult health priorities include prevention—screenings, vaccinations, healthy diet, activity and avoiding tobacco—and management of chronic disease. Normal aging brings gradual changes: decreased muscle mass and bone density, slower reaction time, reduced kidney function (affecting medication clearance), thinner skin, decreased thirst sensation, reduced vision and hearing (**presbyopia** and **presbycusis**) and changes in sleep. These changes raise risks of **falls**, **dehydration**, **pressure injuries** and **adverse drug effects**, especially with **polypharmacy** (many medications). Nurses use fall-risk assessments, review medications, encourage mobility and adapt teaching with large print, good lighting and extra time.',
'Distinguish **delirium** from **dementia**. **Delirium** is an **acute, fluctuating** change in attention and awareness, often caused by infection, dehydration, medications, surgery or electrolyte imbalance; it is a medical emergency and is often reversible. **Dementia** is a **gradual, progressive** decline in memory and thinking, such as Alzheimer disease. **Depression** in older adults can mimic dementia. Sudden confusion is never assumed to be “just old age.”',
sort('Delirium or dementia?',['Delirium','Dementia'],[['Sudden onset over hours to days',0],['Fluctuates during the day',0],['Often reversible when the cause is treated',0],['Gradual onset over months to years',1],['Progressive memory loss',1],['Alzheimer disease is the most common cause',1]])]],
['Mental health nursing',[
'Mental health care begins with **therapeutic communication**: active listening, open-ended questions, reflecting feelings, clarifying and silence, while avoiding false reassurance, judgment or changing the subject. Nurses assess mood, thoughts, behavior and safety, always asking directly about **suicidal thoughts**—asking does not plant the idea. A patient at risk is placed on appropriate observation and the environment made safe. **Anxiety** is managed with a calm approach, simple directions and coping techniques; **depression** with safety, activity and support; and **psychosis** by not arguing with delusions while presenting reality. Common medications include antidepressants (such as SSRIs), antipsychotics, mood stabilizers (lithium requires blood level monitoring) and anxiolytics. Patients have rights to the least restrictive care, and restraints or seclusion are used only for imminent danger under strict rules.',
match('Therapeutic or nontherapeutic?',[['“Tell me more about how you are feeling.”','Open-ended question (therapeutic)'],['“Don’t worry, everything will be fine.”','False reassurance (nontherapeutic)'],['“You seem upset.”','Reflecting feelings (therapeutic)'],['“Why did you do that?”','Demanding explanation (nontherapeutic)'],['Sitting quietly with the patient','Using silence (therapeutic)'],['“Let’s talk about something happier.”','Changing the subject (nontherapeutic)']])]],
['Home care, extended care and rehabilitation',[
'Care continues beyond the hospital. **Home health care** provides skilled nursing, therapy and aide services at home; nurses assess safety, teach self-management and coordinate equipment such as oxygen. **Extended care** facilities (skilled nursing and long-term care) serve people needing ongoing assistance. **Rehabilitation** helps patients regain function after stroke, injury or surgery through physical, occupational and speech therapy, with nurses reinforcing mobility, self-care and adaptive equipment. Discharge planning starts at admission and includes teaching, medication reconciliation and follow-up appointments.']],
['Palliative care, hospice and grief',[
'**Palliative care** relieves symptoms and stress at any stage of serious illness, alongside curative treatment. **Hospice** is palliative care for people with a life expectancy of about **six months or less** who are no longer seeking cure, focused on comfort, dignity and family support. Nurses manage pain, shortness of breath, nausea and anxiety, provide mouth and skin care, and support families. Signs of approaching death include decreased intake, increased sleep, mottled cool extremities, changes in breathing such as **Cheyne-Stokes respirations** and noisy respirations from secretions. **Advance directives** and **do-not-resuscitate orders** guide care. Kübler-Ross described common grief responses—**denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance**—which people move through in varied order.']],
['Postmortem care (HOSA Skill V)',[
'Postmortem care treats the body with respect and prepares it for the family and funeral home, following facility policy and cultural or religious preferences. In Skill V, after hand hygiene, checking the order and assembling supplies, the competitor puts on **one or two pairs of gloves and a protective gown** and confirms identity with **two identifiers**. They **straighten the body** with a small pillow under the head; if the eyes are to be donated, they close them and place a small ice pack on each eye (otherwise they verbalize that eyes are not being donated). **Jewelry and belongings** are removed, placed in a property bag and documented. The mouth is closed with a **chin strap or rolled towel**. All **IV lines, monitors and equipment are removed** unless ordered otherwise (for example, when an autopsy is planned). Linens are removed except the covering sheet, soiled areas are **bathed**, a clean incontinence pad placed, soiled dressings replaced and **wrists and ankles padded** with gauze. **Dentures or glasses are labeled**.',
'**Identification tags** are attached to the **right great toe**, the **wrist or hand** and the **covering sheet**, the body is placed in a **zippered bag**, and any known **communicable disease** is noted on the bag. The competitor removes and disposes of PPE, performs hand hygiene, uses respectful communication and **documents and reports** the procedure. Families may wish to view the body before it is bagged; the nurse prepares the room and offers privacy and support.',
order('Postmortem care (HOSA Skill V)',['Hand hygiene, check order, gloves and gown','Confirm identity with two identifiers','Straighten the body and place a small pillow under the head','Remove and document jewelry and belongings','Close the mouth with a chin strap or rolled towel','Remove lines and equipment unless ordered otherwise','Bathe soiled areas, place a clean pad and pad wrists and ankles','Tag the right great toe, wrist and covering sheet; place in a zippered bag','Remove PPE, hand hygiene, document and report'])]],
["Patient rights, restraints and elder abuse",["Patients in every setting keep their **rights**: dignity, privacy, information, participation in care decisions, refusal of treatment and freedom from abuse and unnecessary restraint. In long-term care, federal regulations reinforce residents’ rights to make choices about daily routines and to voice grievances without retaliation. **Restraints**—physical devices or medications that limit movement—are a last resort after alternatives such as frequent rounding, bed alarms, low beds, diversion and having family present. When ordered, restraints require a provider order with a time limit, the least restrictive type, a quick-release knot tied to the **bed frame (not the side rail)**, room for **two fingers** under the device, and monitoring of circulation, skin, nutrition, hydration and toileting at least every two hours, with release and range of motion per policy.","**Elder abuse** includes physical, emotional, sexual and financial abuse and **neglect**. Warning signs include unexplained bruises in different healing stages, poor hygiene, malnutrition, fear of a caregiver, sudden financial changes and missed medications. Nurses document objective findings and report suspected abuse to adult protective services according to state law. **Advance directives**—a **living will** and a **durable power of attorney for health care**—let adults state their wishes before a crisis, and the Patient Self-Determination Act requires facilities to ask about them on admission.",{"t":"check","q":"When a restraint is ordered, where is it tied?","options":["To the bed frame with a quick-release knot","To the side rail with a tight knot","To the mattress","To the headboard with a square knot"],"why":"Side rails move and can injure the patient; use the frame and a quick-release knot."}]]
],takeaways:['Older adults face falls, dehydration and polypharmacy; delirium is sudden and often reversible, dementia gradual.','Therapeutic communication uses open questions, reflection and silence; always ask directly about suicide.','Hospice is comfort care for about six months or less; Skill V: tag right great toe, wrist and sheet, remove lines unless ordered, pad wrists and ankles, label dentures.'],terms:[['Delirium','Acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention and awareness.'],['Polypharmacy','Use of many medications, increasing risk of adverse effects.'],['Hospice','Comfort-focused care for people with limited life expectancy.']],mcqs:[q('Which condition has a sudden onset and is often reversible?','Delirium','Dementia','Alzheimer disease','Presbycusis','Find and treat the cause.'),q('Which is a therapeutic response?','“Tell me more about how you are feeling.”','“Don’t worry, it will be fine.”','“Why did you do that?”','“Let’s change the subject.”','Open-ended questions invite sharing.'),q('Hospice care is generally for patients with a life expectancy of about:','Six months or less','Two years','One week','Ten years','Hospice focuses on comfort.'),q('In postmortem care, where is one identification tag attached?','The right great toe','The left ear','The forehead','The bed rail only','Tags also go on the wrist and sheet.'),q('If the person’s eyes are to be donated, the nurse should:','Close the eyes and place a small ice pack on each','Leave the eyes open','Apply eye ointment','Tape the eyes shut tightly','Cooling preserves tissue.'),q('Why are dentures labeled in postmortem care?','So funeral home staff can identify them','To discard them','To return them to the hospital','To clean them','Labeling prevents loss.'),q('Which breathing pattern may occur near death?','Cheyne-Stokes respirations','Kussmaul only','Normal regular breathing always','Hyperventilation only','Cycles of deep breathing and apnea.'),q('Polypharmacy increases the risk of:','Adverse drug effects and interactions','Better adherence','Lower costs','Improved kidney function','More drugs mean more interactions.')],frq:{prompt:'An 84-year-old hospice patient dies peacefully with family present. Describe end-of-life nursing support for the family, the postmortem care steps from HOSA Skill V, and how cultural and religious preferences are respected.',rubric:[['Family support and grief',['family','privacy','support','grief','view']],['Skill V preparation steps',['gloves','gown','two identifiers','straighten','pillow','chin strap']],['Belongings, lines and tags',['jewelry','property','lines','toe','wrist','sheet','zippered','dentures']],['Culture and documentation',['cultur','religio','preference','document','report']]],model:'The nurse offers the family privacy and time with their loved one, provides emotional support and information, contacts chaplain or social work if desired and recognizes that grief responses vary. For postmortem care, the nurse performs hand hygiene, checks the order, puts on gloves and a gown and confirms identity with two identifiers, straightens the body with a small pillow under the head, closes the eyes (with ice packs if eyes are to be donated) and closes the mouth with a chin strap or rolled towel. Jewelry and belongings are removed and documented in a property bag, lines and equipment are removed unless ordered otherwise, soiled areas are bathed, a clean pad is placed, wrists and ankles are padded and dentures or glasses are labeled. Identification tags are attached to the right great toe, the wrist and the covering sheet, the body is placed in a zippered bag with any communicable disease noted, PPE is removed, hand hygiene performed and the procedure documented and reported. Throughout, the nurse asks about and honors cultural and religious practices, such as rituals, who may touch the body and timing.'},sources:[RULES,NIA_ELDER,NIMH,NIA_HOSPICE]};

export const N8={title:'Cardiovascular and respiratory disorders',hook:'Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States, and chronic lung disease is among the leading causes. Nurses meet these conditions everywhere: the patient with crushing chest pain, the patient with heart failure gaining fluid, the patient with COPD who cannot catch a breath. Systems disorders make up 60% of the Clinical Nursing test.',objectives:['Relate cardiovascular and respiratory structure and function to common disorders.','Recognize signs and symptoms and perform focused nursing assessments.','Describe medications and nursing interventions for hypertension, coronary artery disease, heart failure, COPD, asthma and pneumonia.','Provide safe oxygen therapy and patient teaching.'],sections:[
['Heart and vessels',[
'The heart pumps blood through the pulmonary and systemic circulations, and the **coronary arteries** supply its muscle. **Hypertension** (sustained blood pressure of 130/80 mm Hg or higher by current U.S. guidelines) damages vessels and organs and is often silent; management includes lifestyle changes (sodium reduction, exercise, weight loss) and medications such as **ACE inhibitors**, **beta blockers**, **calcium channel blockers** and **diuretics**. **Coronary artery disease** narrows coronary arteries with atherosclerotic plaque. **Angina** is chest pain from reduced blood flow, relieved by rest or **nitroglycerin**. A **myocardial infarction (MI)** occurs when an artery is blocked; signs include chest pressure that may radiate to the arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath, sweating and nausea—women and people with diabetes may have atypical symptoms such as fatigue. Emergency care includes calling for help, oxygen if saturation is low, aspirin as ordered, nitroglycerin, an ECG and rapid reperfusion. Troponin confirms heart muscle injury.',
{t:'w',kind:'label',diagram:'heart',title:'Explore the heart',parts:[['ra','Right atrium','Receives deoxygenated blood from the body.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs; right-sided failure causes peripheral edema.'],['la','Left atrium','Receives oxygenated blood from the lungs.'],['lv','Left ventricle','Pumps blood to the body; left-sided failure causes pulmonary congestion.'],['aorta','Aorta','Carries blood from the left ventricle to the body.'],['mitral','Mitral valve','Between left atrium and left ventricle.'],['aortic','Aortic valve','Between left ventricle and aorta.']]}]],
['Heart failure',[
'**Heart failure** means the heart cannot pump enough blood to meet the body’s needs. **Left-sided failure** backs blood into the lungs, causing **dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles and cough** with frothy sputum. **Right-sided failure** backs blood into the body, causing **peripheral edema, jugular vein distension, weight gain, liver enlargement and ascites**. Nursing care includes **daily weights** (a gain of more than about 2–3 pounds in a day or 5 pounds in a week is reported), intake and output, **sodium and fluid restriction** as ordered, elevating the head of the bed, monitoring lung sounds and oxygen saturation, and medications such as **diuretics** (monitor potassium), ACE inhibitors or ARBs, beta blockers and sometimes **digoxin** (check the apical pulse for a full minute and hold if below the ordered limit, often 60; watch for toxicity such as nausea and visual changes).',
sort('Left-sided or right-sided heart failure?',['Left-sided','Right-sided'],[['Crackles in the lungs',0],['Orthopnea',0],['Pink, frothy sputum',0],['Pitting edema of the legs',1],['Jugular vein distension',1],['Enlarged liver and ascites',1]])]],
['Respiratory structure and disorders',[
'Air moves through the upper airway, trachea and bronchi to the **alveoli**, where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged. **Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**—chronic bronchitis and emphysema, usually from smoking—causes airflow limitation, chronic cough and dyspnea; patients may use **pursed-lip breathing** and sit in the **tripod position**. Care includes bronchodilators, inhaled steroids, smoking cessation, vaccines and pulmonary rehabilitation. **Asthma** involves reversible airway inflammation and bronchospasm with wheezing; **short-acting beta agonists** such as albuterol are rescue medications, and inhaled corticosteroids control inflammation. **Pneumonia** is lung infection with fever, productive cough, crackles and low oxygen saturation; nurses encourage coughing and deep breathing, fluids, positioning and antibiotics as ordered. **Pulmonary embolism** causes sudden dyspnea, chest pain and anxiety and is an emergency.',
{t:'w',kind:'label',diagram:'resp',title:'Explore the respiratory tract',parts:[['trachea','Trachea','Airway from the larynx to the bronchi.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Narrow in asthma from bronchospasm and inflammation.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Small airways obstructed in COPD.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Gas exchange sites destroyed in emphysema and filled with fluid in pneumonia.'],['pleura','Pleura','Membranes around the lungs; air here causes pneumothorax.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Main breathing muscle.']]}]],
['Oxygen therapy and respiratory care',[
'Oxygen is a medication requiring an order. A **nasal cannula** delivers **1–6 L/min** (roughly 24–44% oxygen); a **simple face mask** about 5–10 L/min; a **non-rebreather mask** at 10–15 L/min delivers high concentrations in emergencies; a **Venturi mask** delivers precise percentages, useful in COPD. Target saturations are commonly **92–98%**, and often **88–92%** for patients with COPD who retain carbon dioxide, as ordered. Safety: no smoking or open flames, secure cylinders, check skin under tubing for pressure, and humidify at higher flows. Nurses also position patients upright, encourage incentive spirometry, suction as needed and teach correct inhaler technique (shake, exhale, inhale slowly while pressing, hold breath about 10 seconds; rinse mouth after inhaled steroids).',
"Teaching is a large part of cardiopulmonary care. Patients with heart disease learn to take medications at the same time each day, check their pulse when ordered, weigh themselves every morning after voiding and before breakfast, limit sodium (often to about 2,000 mg a day as ordered), read food labels and recognize warning signs that need a call to the provider. **Cardiac rehabilitation** combines supervised exercise, education and emotional support after a heart attack, surgery or heart failure diagnosis. Patients with lung disease learn energy conservation, airway clearance, how to use home oxygen safely and when to start their action plan. **Smoking cessation** is the single most effective step for both groups, and annual influenza and recommended pneumococcal vaccines reduce dangerous infections.",
match('Match the device to its typical flow',[['Nasal cannula','1–6 L/min'],['Simple face mask','5–10 L/min'],['Non-rebreather mask','10–15 L/min'],['Venturi mask','Precise oxygen percentage'],['Rescue inhaler','Albuterol (short-acting beta agonist)'],['Rinse mouth afterward','Inhaled corticosteroid']])]],
['Applying clinical judgment',[
kase('Shortness of breath at night',{a:{text:'A patient with heart failure gained 4 pounds since yesterday, needs three pillows to breathe and has crackles at both lung bases. What is happening?',choices:[['Fluid volume excess from worsening left-sided heart failure','b','Correct. Weight gain, orthopnea and crackles indicate fluid overload.',true],['Dehydration','b','Dehydration causes weight loss and dry membranes.',false]]},b:{text:'What should the nurse do first?',choices:[['Raise the head of the bed, check oxygen saturation and notify the provider','c','Correct. Improve breathing and escalate.',true],['Encourage the patient to drink more water','c','Fluids would worsen overload.',false]]},c:{text:'The provider orders an IV diuretic. The nurse monitors urine output, potassium, weight and lung sounds, and the patient breathes more easily by evening.',end:true}})]],
["Cardiac diagnostics and peripheral vascular disease",["An **electrocardiogram (ECG)** records the heart’s electrical activity: the **P wave** is atrial depolarization, the **QRS complex** ventricular depolarization and the **T wave** ventricular repolarization. Nurses recognize normal sinus rhythm (60–100 beats/min), **bradycardia**, **tachycardia** and **atrial fibrillation** (irregularly irregular, raising stroke risk) and respond immediately to **ventricular fibrillation** and pulseless ventricular tachycardia with CPR and defibrillation. Blood tests include **troponin** for heart muscle damage, a **lipid panel** (LDL “bad,” HDL “good,” triglycerides), **BNP** for heart failure and **PT/INR** for patients on warfarin. Cardiac catheterization patients are monitored for bleeding at the puncture site and distal pulses.",{"t":"w","kind":"ecg","title":"Compare cardiac rhythms","prompt":"Compare normal sinus rhythm with bradycardia, tachycardia, atrial fibrillation and ventricular fibrillation. Which require immediate action?"},"Circulation problems also affect the limbs. **Peripheral arterial disease (PAD)** reduces blood flow to the legs, causing **pain when walking (intermittent claudication)**, cool pale skin, weak pulses, hair loss and slow-healing wounds on the toes; legs are kept **dependent** (not elevated) and patients are encouraged to walk. **Venous insufficiency** causes swelling, brown discoloration and ulcers near the ankles; legs are **elevated** and compression stockings used. **Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)** causes unilateral calf swelling, warmth and pain and can travel to the lungs as a **pulmonary embolism**. Prevention includes early ambulation, leg exercises, sequential compression devices and anticoagulants as ordered; patients on anticoagulants are taught bleeding precautions.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Arterial or venous problem?","buckets":["Arterial (PAD)","Venous"],"items":[["Pain with walking relieved by rest",0],["Cool, pale skin and weak pulses",0],["Keep legs dependent",0],["Ankle swelling and brown discoloration",1],["Elevate the legs",1],["Unilateral calf swelling and warmth (DVT)",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Hypertension ≥130/80; angina is relieved by rest or nitroglycerin; MI needs rapid recognition, ECG and troponin.','Left-sided HF: lungs (crackles, orthopnea); right-sided: body (edema, JVD); daily weights and diuretics with potassium monitoring.','COPD: pursed-lip breathing, often SpO₂ 88–92% target; asthma: albuterol rescue; cannula 1–6 L/min, non-rebreather 10–15 L/min.'],terms:[['Orthopnea','Difficulty breathing when lying flat.'],['Angina','Chest pain from reduced coronary blood flow.'],['Non-rebreather mask','High-flow oxygen mask with a reservoir bag.']],mcqs:[q('Crackles and orthopnea are signs of:','Left-sided heart failure','Right-sided heart failure','Dehydration','Hypoglycemia','Fluid backs into the lungs.'),q('Before giving digoxin, the nurse checks:','The apical pulse for a full minute','The blood glucose','The temperature','The pupil size','Hold if below the ordered limit.'),q('A nasal cannula typically delivers oxygen at:','1–6 L/min','10–15 L/min','20–30 L/min','0.1 L/min','Higher flows use masks.'),q('Which medication is a rescue inhaler for asthma?','Albuterol','Inhaled corticosteroid','Digoxin','Furosemide','Short-acting beta agonists relieve bronchospasm.'),q('Which finding should be reported for a heart failure patient?','Weight gain of 3 pounds in one day','Stable weight','Clear lungs','Normal urine output','Rapid gain indicates fluid retention.'),q('Pursed-lip breathing helps patients with:','COPD','Hypoglycemia','Appendicitis','Fractures','It keeps airways open longer.'),q('Angina is typically relieved by:','Rest or nitroglycerin','Eating','Lying flat','Cold water','It results from reduced blood flow.'),q('A common oxygen saturation target for many COPD patients who retain CO₂ is:','88–92%','100%','75–80%','95–100% always','Follow the provider’s order.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare the nursing assessment and care of a patient with left-sided heart failure and a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation, including key signs, medications and oxygen therapy.',rubric:[['Heart failure signs',['crackles','orthopnea','dyspnea','weight']],['Heart failure care and meds',['daily weight','diuretic','potassium','sodium','fluid restriction','digoxin']],['COPD signs and care',['pursed-lip','tripod','bronchodilator','wheez','smoking']],['Oxygen therapy',['88','92','venturi','cannula','order']]],model:'A patient with left-sided heart failure shows dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles and sometimes frothy sputum, with weight gain from fluid retention; care includes daily weights, intake and output, sodium and fluid restriction, elevating the head of the bed, monitoring lung sounds and saturation, and giving diuretics while monitoring potassium, plus ACE inhibitors, beta blockers or digoxin as ordered. A patient with a COPD exacerbation has worsening dyspnea, wheezing, increased sputum, pursed-lip breathing and may sit in the tripod position; care includes bronchodilators, inhaled or systemic steroids, antibiotics if infection is present, upright positioning, breathing techniques and smoking cessation teaching. Oxygen is given as ordered; for many COPD patients who retain carbon dioxide, the target saturation is about 88–92%, often using a Venturi mask or low-flow nasal cannula, while heart failure patients are usually titrated to the ordered target such as 92–98%.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_HF,NHLBI_COPD]};

export const N9={title:'Neurological, endocrine, digestive and urinary disorders',hook:'A patient’s face droops and speech slurs; another is shaky and sweating with a glucose of 48 mg/dL; a third has not urinated in eight hours after surgery. Recognizing a stroke, a hypoglycemic reaction or urinary retention quickly—and responding correctly—is core nursing. HOSA’s straight catheterization skill applies sterile technique to the last of these.',objectives:['Recognize stroke with BE FAST and assess level of consciousness with the Glasgow Coma Scale.','Distinguish hypoglycemia from hyperglycemia and describe diabetes management.','Describe urinary tract infection, retention and kidney disease and prevention of CAUTI.','Perform straight urinary catheterization following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['Neurological disorders',[
'A **stroke** (cerebrovascular accident) occurs when blood flow to the brain is blocked (**ischemic**, most common) or a vessel bleeds (**hemorrhagic**). Recognize it with **BE FAST**: **B**alance loss, **E**yes (vision changes), **F**ace drooping, **A**rm weakness, **S**peech difficulty and **T**ime to call emergency services and note the time of onset—treatments that dissolve or remove clots are time-sensitive. Nursing care includes neurological checks, swallow screening before oral intake, positioning, fall prevention and rehabilitation. **Seizure** care focuses on safety: protect the head, turn the patient on the side, never put anything in the mouth, time the seizure and observe. Level of consciousness is assessed with the **Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)**, scoring eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5) and motor response (1–6) for a total of **3–15**; a score of 8 or less often indicates the need to protect the airway. Pupils are checked for size, equality and reaction (PERRLA).',
{t:'w',kind:'gcs',title:'Score the Glasgow Coma Scale',prompt:'Select the best eye, verbal and motor responses to calculate the total.'}]],
['Diabetes and endocrine disorders',[
'**Diabetes mellitus** causes high blood glucose. **Type 1** results from autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing beta cells and requires insulin; **type 2** involves insulin resistance and is managed with lifestyle changes, oral medications such as **metformin**, and sometimes insulin. Classic hyperglycemia symptoms are the “three Ps”—**polyuria, polydipsia and polyphagia**—plus fatigue and blurred vision. **Hypoglycemia** (often below 70 mg/dL) causes **shakiness, sweating, rapid heartbeat, confusion and irritability**; a conscious patient is treated with **15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate**, glucose rechecked in 15 minutes (the **15-15 rule**), and an unconscious patient receives glucagon or IV dextrose. Remember “**cold and clammy, need some candy**; **hot and dry, sugar high**.” **Diabetic ketoacidosis**, mostly in type 1, causes high glucose, ketones, fruity breath and deep **Kussmaul respirations**. Nurses check glucose before meals, coordinate insulin with meals, teach foot care and monitor for complications. **Hypothyroidism** causes fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation; **hyperthyroidism** causes weight loss, heat intolerance, rapid heart rate and anxiety.',
{t:'w',kind:'glucose',title:'Watch glucose and insulin interact',prompt:'Compare a healthy body with type 1 and type 2 diabetes after a meal.'},
sort('Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia?',['Hypoglycemia','Hyperglycemia'],[['Shaky, sweaty and confused',0],['Cold, clammy skin',0],['Treated with 15 g fast-acting carbohydrate',0],['Polyuria and polydipsia',1],['Fruity breath and Kussmaul respirations',1],['Hot, dry skin',1]])]],
['Renal and urinary disorders',[
'The kidneys filter blood, balance fluid and electrolytes, regulate blood pressure and make erythropoietin. **Urinary tract infections (UTIs)** cause burning, frequency, urgency and cloudy urine; older adults may present with confusion instead. Prevention includes adequate fluids, wiping front to back and voiding after intercourse. **Urinary retention** (inability to empty the bladder) after surgery or with some medications causes distension and discomfort and may require catheterization. **Acute kidney injury** develops quickly from low blood flow, toxins or obstruction; **chronic kidney disease** progresses over years, often from diabetes and hypertension, causing fluid overload, hyperkalemia, anemia and the need for **dialysis** or transplant. Nurses monitor I&O, daily weights, BUN, creatinine and potassium and protect any **dialysis access** (no blood pressures or needle sticks in the arm with a fistula; feel for a thrill and listen for a bruit).',
'**Catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTIs)** are common, preventable HAIs. Prevention: insert catheters only when necessary, use sterile technique, keep the drainage bag **below the bladder** and off the floor, maintain a closed system, perform daily perineal care and **remove the catheter as soon as possible**. Intermittent (straight) catheterization often carries lower infection risk than an indwelling catheter for retention.']],
['Straight urinary catheterization (HOSA Skill III)',[
'In Skill III, the competitor performs hand hygiene and applies gloves, assembles supplies, checks the order, greets and identifies the patient by ID band and name, assesses mobility and cooperation, **asks about allergies to antiseptics such as povidone-iodine and to latex**, explains and provides privacy, raises the bed and lowers the near side rail. The patient is positioned: **female—supine with knees flexed and feet apart**; **male—supine with thighs slightly abducted**. A waterproof pad is placed and the patient draped, with a light positioned as needed. With clean gloves, the perineal area is washed, rinsed and dried and the **urinary meatus identified**; gloves are removed and hand hygiene performed. A biohazard bag is prepared, the **catheterization kit opened** according to directions and **sterile gloves** put on. The perineum is draped while keeping gloves and drape sterile, supplies organized on the sterile field, the specimen container opened if needed, antiseptic applied to cotton balls or swabs, and the catheter **lubricated 1–2 inches for women and 5–7 inches for men**.',
'For a **female**, the nondominant hand spreads the labia to expose the meatus and **stays in place** (it is now contaminated); with forceps and a new antiseptic cotton ball for each stroke, the nurse cleans **from the clitoris toward the anus**—far side, near side, then directly over the meatus—in single downward motions. For a **male**, the nondominant hand holds the penis below the glans throughout, and the meatus is cleaned in a circular motion three times with a new cotton ball each time. The catheter is held with the sterile dominant hand 3–4 inches from the tip, with the other end in the collection tray or specimen cup, and inserted gently until urine flows (about 2–3 inches in women, 7–9 inches in men), then advanced slightly further. The bladder is drained as ordered, a specimen collected if needed, the catheter removed, the patient cleaned and made comfortable, supplies discarded, hand hygiene performed and the volume and characteristics of urine documented.',
order('Straight catheterization, female (HOSA Skill III)',['Hand hygiene, check order, identify patient and ask about allergies','Position supine with knees flexed and feet apart; drape','Clean the perineum with clean gloves and identify the meatus','Hand hygiene, open the kit and put on sterile gloves','Drape, organize supplies and lubricate 1–2 inches','Spread labia with nondominant hand and keep it in place','Clean far side, near side and center front to back with new swabs','Insert the catheter until urine flows; drain and collect specimen','Remove the catheter, clean up and document'])]],
['Applying clinical judgment',[
kase('A shaky patient before lunch',{a:{text:'A patient with diabetes who received insulin is sweaty, shaky and confused before lunch. Glucose is 52 mg/dL and he can swallow. What first?',choices:[['Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate such as juice, then recheck in 15 minutes','b','Correct. Follow the 15-15 rule for a conscious patient.',true],['Give his scheduled insulin','b','Insulin would lower glucose further.',false]]},b:{text:'Fifteen minutes later glucose is 84 mg/dL. What next?',choices:[['Give a snack or meal with protein and complex carbohydrate and report the episode','c','Correct. This prevents recurrence; the provider may adjust insulin.',true],['Nothing further is needed','c','Without food, glucose may drop again.',false]]},c:{text:'The patient eats lunch, the nurse documents the event and the provider reviews the insulin dose.',end:true}})]],
["Gastrointestinal and liver disorders",["The digestive system breaks down food, absorbs nutrients and eliminates waste. **Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)** causes heartburn; patients avoid large meals and lying down after eating and elevate the head of the bed. **Peptic ulcers** are often caused by ***Helicobacter pylori*** or NSAIDs; black, tarry stools (**melena**) or vomiting blood signal bleeding. **Appendicitis** begins with pain around the umbilicus that moves to the **right lower quadrant**; sudden relief of pain may mean rupture and peritonitis. **Bowel obstruction** causes distension, cramping, vomiting and absent stools and may require an NG tube for decompression. **Inflammatory bowel disease** (Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis) causes chronic diarrhea and may lead to an **ostomy**, whose stoma should be **moist and pink to red**—a dark or pale stoma is reported.","**Hepatitis** is liver inflammation, often viral: hepatitis A spreads by the fecal–oral route, while hepatitis B and C spread through blood and body fluids. **Cirrhosis** scars the liver, causing **jaundice**, ascites, bleeding tendencies and confusion from ammonia build-up (hepatic encephalopathy). Nurses monitor abdominal girth, weight, mental status and bleeding and teach avoidance of alcohol and liver-toxic medications such as excess acetaminophen.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the GI finding to its likely cause","pairs":[["Right lower quadrant pain with rebound tenderness","Appendicitis"],["Black, tarry stools","Upper GI bleeding"],["Heartburn after large meals","GERD"],["Distension with vomiting and no stool","Bowel obstruction"],["Yellow skin and sclera","Jaundice from liver disease"],["Dark purple stoma","Poor stoma blood supply—report"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Stroke: BE FAST and note time of onset; GCS 3–15 (eye 4, verbal 5, motor 6); seizures: protect, side-lying, nothing in mouth.','Hypoglycemia: shaky, sweaty, confused—15 g carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes; DKA: ketones, fruity breath, Kussmaul.','Prevent CAUTI: necessity, sterile insertion, bag below bladder, early removal; Skill III: lubricate 1–2 in (F) / 5–7 in (M), clean front to back with new swabs.'],terms:[['BE FAST','Balance, Eyes, Face, Arm, Speech, Time—stroke warning signs.'],['Glasgow Coma Scale','Level-of-consciousness score from 3 to 15.'],['CAUTI','Catheter-associated urinary tract infection.']],mcqs:[q('In BE FAST, the “T” stands for:','Time to call emergency services','Temperature','Tongue','Treatment','Treatments are time-sensitive.'),q('The Glasgow Coma Scale ranges from:','3 to 15','0 to 10','1 to 5','10 to 20','Eye, verbal and motor scores add up.'),q('A conscious patient with glucose 52 mg/dL should receive:','15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate','Insulin','Nothing by mouth','A large meal only','Follow the 15-15 rule.'),q('Kussmaul respirations and fruity breath suggest:','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Hypoglycemia','Stroke','Hypothyroidism','Ketones cause these signs.'),q('During female catheterization, cleaning is done:','From the clitoris toward the anus with a new swab each stroke','From the anus toward the clitoris','In circles with one swab','With clean gloves only','Front to back prevents contamination.'),q('How much of the catheter is lubricated for a male in Skill III?','5–7 inches','1–2 inches','10–12 inches','Only the tip','Women need 1–2 inches.'),q('Which action helps prevent CAUTI?','Keep the drainage bag below the bladder','Hang the bag above the bed','Leave the catheter in as long as possible','Disconnect the tubing often','Gravity prevents backflow.'),q('During a seizure, the nurse should:','Protect the head, turn the patient on the side and time the seizure','Put a tongue blade in the mouth','Restrain the limbs','Give water','Nothing goes in the mouth.')],frq:{prompt:'A postoperative patient has not voided for eight hours and has a distended bladder. Explain how you would assess and manage urinary retention, perform a straight catheterization following HOSA Skill III for a female patient, and prevent infection.',rubric:[['Assessment of retention',['distend','palpat','bladder scan','output','discomfort']],['Preparation and safety',['order','identify','allerg','latex','iodine','privacy','position']],['Sterile technique and cleansing',['sterile gloves','1-2','1–2','labia','front to back','new swab','nondominant']],['Insertion, documentation and infection prevention',['urine','drain','document','sterile','cauti','remove']]],model:'I would assess for bladder distension by palpation or bladder scanner, ask about discomfort and review intake, output and medications, then notify the provider and obtain an order. After checking the order, identifying the patient, asking about allergies to iodine and latex, explaining and providing privacy, I would position her supine with knees flexed and feet apart, drape her and clean the perineum with clean gloves to identify the meatus. After hand hygiene, I would open the kit, put on sterile gloves, drape, organize supplies and lubricate 1–2 inches of the catheter. With my nondominant hand spreading the labia and staying in place, I would clean from the clitoris toward the anus on the far side, near side and over the meatus using a new swab each time, then insert the catheter until urine flows, drain the bladder, collect any specimen, remove the catheter and document the amount and characteristics. Using strict sterile technique, intermittent rather than indwelling catheterization when possible and encouraging fluids and regular voiding help prevent infection.'},sources:[RULES,ASA_STROKE,NIDDK_DM,CDC_CAUTI]};

export const N10={title:'Musculoskeletal, integumentary, immune and oncology care',hook:'A teenager in a new cast complains that her fingers are numb and her pain is worsening despite medication. A burn patient’s fluid needs are calculated by body surface area. A patient on chemotherapy develops a fever. Each situation requires the nurse to recognize a complication early and act.',objectives:['Care for patients with fractures, casts and traction, and recognize compartment syndrome.','Assess burns and wounds and describe skin disorders and burn care.','Recognize sepsis and allergic reactions and provide immune-related care.','Manage common side effects of cancer treatment and protect immunocompromised patients.'],sections:[
['Fractures, casts and mobility',[
'Fractures are treated by **reduction** (realigning bone) and **immobilization** with splints, casts, traction or surgical fixation. Nursing care includes **neurovascular checks** of the affected extremity—the **five Ps** (sometimes six): **pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia** (numbness or tingling) and **paralysis**, plus poikilothermia (coolness). **Compartment syndrome** is an emergency in which swelling within a muscle compartment cuts off circulation; the earliest sign is often **pain out of proportion to the injury that is not relieved by medication**, especially with passive stretching. The nurse elevates the limb to heart level (not above), notifies the provider immediately and may need to loosen dressings or bivalve the cast. **Cast care**: elevate for the first 24–48 hours, keep it dry, do not insert objects to scratch, and report odor, drainage or tightness. **Osteoporosis** increases fracture risk, especially hip fractures in older adults; prevention includes calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise and fall prevention. Safe mobility uses gait belts, proper body mechanics and assistive devices.',
sort('Expected or report immediately?',['Expected after casting','Report immediately'],[['Mild swelling that improves with elevation',0],['Pain controlled by ordered medication',0],['Numbness and tingling in the fingers',1],['Severe pain unrelieved by medication',1],['Pale, cool fingers with slow capillary refill',1],['Cast feels heavy and warm while drying',0]])]],
['Skin and burns',[
'The skin protects against infection and fluid loss. **Burns** are classified by depth: **superficial** (first-degree; epidermis; red and painful, like sunburn), **partial-thickness** (second-degree; into the dermis; blisters, very painful) and **full-thickness** (third-degree; through the dermis; white, leathery or charred, may be painless because nerves are destroyed). The **rule of nines** estimates total body surface area (TBSA) burned in adults: head 9%, each arm 9%, front of trunk 18%, back of trunk 18%, each leg 18% and perineum 1%. Major burns cause massive fluid shifts; fluid resuscitation is calculated by formulas such as Parkland based on weight and TBSA. Priorities are airway (especially with smoke inhalation or facial burns), fluid replacement, pain control, infection prevention and nutrition. **Skin disorders** include **cellulitis** (spreading bacterial skin infection with redness, warmth and swelling), **herpes zoster** (shingles, a painful vesicular rash along a dermatome) and **pressure injuries**.',
{t:'w',kind:'burns',title:'Estimate burn size with the rule of nines',prompt:'Select burned regions to calculate total body surface area.'}]],
['Immune responses, allergy and sepsis',[
'**Allergic reactions** range from mild (rash, itching) to **anaphylaxis**—a life-threatening reaction with hives, swelling of the lips and throat, wheezing, hypotension and shock. The nurse stops the trigger (such as a medication infusion), calls for help, maintains the airway, and gives **epinephrine intramuscularly** as ordered or per protocol, along with oxygen, IV fluids and other medications. Allergies are documented and patients wear allergy bands. **Sepsis** is a life-threatening response to infection that damages organs. Warning signs include fever or low temperature, rapid heart rate, rapid breathing, confusion, low blood pressure, and low urine output; early recognition, blood cultures, antibiotics and fluids save lives. **Autoimmune diseases** such as rheumatoid arthritis and lupus are managed with anti-inflammatory and immune-suppressing medications, which raise infection risk.',
kase('A reaction during an infusion',{a:{text:'Ten minutes into an IV antibiotic, a patient develops hives, throat tightness and wheezing. What should the nurse do first?',choices:[['Stop the infusion and call for emergency help','b','Correct. Remove the trigger immediately.',true],['Slow the infusion rate','b','Any continued infusion worsens anaphylaxis.',false]]},b:{text:'Blood pressure falls to 80/46. Which medication is given first per protocol?',choices:[['Intramuscular epinephrine','c','Correct. Epinephrine reverses airway swelling and hypotension.',true],['An oral antihistamine','c','Antihistamines are adjuncts, not first-line for anaphylaxis.',false]]},c:{text:'After epinephrine, oxygen and fluids, the patient stabilizes. The allergy is documented, an allergy band applied and the reaction reported.',end:true}})]],
['Cancer care',[
'Cancer is treated with **surgery**, **chemotherapy**, **radiation**, **immunotherapy**, **targeted therapy** and **hormone therapy**. Chemotherapy affects rapidly dividing cells, causing side effects: **bone marrow suppression**—**neutropenia** (low neutrophils, raising infection risk), **anemia** (fatigue) and **thrombocytopenia** (bleeding risk)—**nausea and vomiting**, **mucositis** (mouth sores), **hair loss** and fatigue. **Neutropenic precautions** include strict hand hygiene, avoiding sick visitors, sometimes avoiding fresh flowers and raw foods, and **reporting any fever promptly**, because a fever in a neutropenic patient is an emergency. **Thrombocytopenia precautions** include soft toothbrushes, electric razors, avoiding injections when possible and watching for bruising and bleeding. Radiation can cause skin irritation in the treated area; patients avoid lotions unless approved, sun exposure and harsh soaps. Pain, nutrition, emotional support and advance care planning are central to oncology nursing.',
match('Match the side effect to the nursing action',[['Neutropenia','Strict hand hygiene and report fever immediately'],['Thrombocytopenia','Soft toothbrush and electric razor'],['Mucositis','Gentle mouth care and soft, bland foods'],['Nausea','Antiemetics before chemotherapy as ordered'],['Radiation skin reaction','Gentle cleansing and avoid sun on the area'],['Anemia','Plan rest periods and monitor fatigue']])]],
['Integrating care across systems',[
'Many patients have problems in several systems at once, so nurses connect findings: an older adult with a hip fracture is at risk of delirium, pneumonia, pressure injuries and blood clots; a patient on chemotherapy with a urinary catheter faces infection from multiple sources. Clinical judgment means prioritizing life-threatening problems (airway, breathing, circulation, sepsis, compartment syndrome), preventing complications through basic care and coordinating with the team. These connections are exactly what the 60% systems portion of the Clinical Nursing test assesses.',
flip('Emergencies to recognize',[['Compartment syndrome','Pain out of proportion, unrelieved by medication'],['Anaphylaxis','Hives, airway swelling, wheezing, hypotension'],['Neutropenic fever','Fever with low neutrophils—report immediately'],['Sepsis','Infection with rapid HR/RR, confusion, hypotension'],['Smoke inhalation','Singed nasal hair, hoarseness, soot—protect airway'],['Hypoglycemia','Shaky, sweaty, confused—give fast carbohydrate']])]],
["Blood disorders and transfusion safety",["**Anemia** is a low red cell count or hemoglobin, causing fatigue, pallor, shortness of breath and a rapid heart rate. **Iron-deficiency anemia** is most common and treated with iron (taken with vitamin C, which can darken stools); **vitamin B12 deficiency** (pernicious anemia) causes neurological symptoms and needs B12 injections. **Sickle cell disease** is an inherited disorder in which red cells sickle and block small vessels, causing painful **vaso-occlusive crises**; care includes hydration, oxygen, pain control and infection prevention. **Hemophilia** is an inherited clotting factor deficiency causing prolonged bleeding and joint bleeding. **Leukemia** produces abnormal white blood cells that crowd out normal blood cells.","**Blood transfusions** require informed consent, a provider order and a matched specimen. Before starting, **two qualified staff verify** the patient’s identity, blood type, unit number and expiration at the bedside. Blood is given through a filtered set with **normal saline only**, started within 30 minutes of arrival from the blood bank and completed within **four hours**. Baseline vital signs are taken, and the nurse **stays with the patient for the first 15 minutes**, when serious reactions are most likely. Signs of a reaction—fever, chills, back pain, hives, wheezing, hypotension or dark urine—mean **stop the transfusion immediately**, keep the IV open with normal saline using new tubing, notify the provider and blood bank, and return the bag and tubing for testing. Circulatory overload from transfusion causes dyspnea and crackles.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Respond to a suspected transfusion reaction","steps":["Stop the transfusion immediately","Keep the IV open with normal saline on new tubing","Check vital signs and stay with the patient","Notify the provider and blood bank","Return the blood bag and tubing for testing","Document the reaction and interventions"]}]]
],takeaways:['Neurovascular checks (five Ps); compartment syndrome pain is unrelieved by medication—elevate to heart level and notify immediately.','Burns: superficial, partial-thickness, full-thickness; rule of nines estimates TBSA; airway and fluids are priorities.','Anaphylaxis: stop the trigger, IM epinephrine; neutropenia: hand hygiene and report fever; thrombocytopenia: bleeding precautions.'],terms:[['Compartment syndrome','Dangerous pressure build-up in a muscle compartment.'],['Rule of nines','Method to estimate burned body surface area.'],['Neutropenia','Low neutrophil count increasing infection risk.']],mcqs:[q('The earliest sign of compartment syndrome is often:','Pain out of proportion that is unrelieved by medication','Warm pink fingers','Itching under the cast','Normal capillary refill','Act immediately.'),q('Using the rule of nines, each adult arm represents:','9%','18%','1%','4.5%','Each leg is 18%.'),q('A full-thickness burn may be:','White or leathery and painless','Red and painful like a sunburn','Blistered and very painful only','Only in the epidermis','Nerve endings are destroyed.'),q('The first-line medication for anaphylaxis is:','Intramuscular epinephrine','Oral antihistamine','Acetaminophen','Insulin','It reverses airway swelling and hypotension.'),q('A patient with neutropenia develops a fever. The nurse should:','Report it immediately','Wait 24 hours','Give a cold shower','Ignore it','Neutropenic fever is an emergency.'),q('Which precaution is for thrombocytopenia?','Use a soft toothbrush and electric razor','Encourage contact sports','Give frequent IM injections','Use a straight razor','Prevent bleeding.'),q('After casting, an extremity should be:','Elevated for the first 24–48 hours','Kept below the heart','Immersed in water','Scratched under the cast','Elevation reduces swelling.'),q('Which signs suggest sepsis?','Fever, rapid heart rate, confusion and low blood pressure','Normal vital signs','Slow heart rate and high blood pressure','Only a mild rash','Sepsis harms organs.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient receiving chemotherapy for leukemia has a fractured forearm in a new cast. Identify the major complications you would monitor for in each problem, explain the assessments and precautions, and describe when you would escalate care.',rubric:[['Cast and neurovascular care',['five ps','neurovascular','numb','pallor','elevat','compartment']],['Chemotherapy side effects and precautions',['neutropenia','thrombocytopenia','hand hygiene','bleeding','soft toothbrush']],['Infection and sepsis monitoring',['fever','sepsis','infection','report']],['Escalation criteria',['immediately','unrelieved','notify','provider','emergency']]],model:'For the cast, I would perform neurovascular checks of the fingers—pain, pallor, pulses, paresthesia and movement, plus temperature and capillary refill—elevate the arm for 24–48 hours, keep the cast dry and teach not to insert objects. Severe pain unrelieved by medication, numbness or pale, cool fingers suggest compartment syndrome, so I would elevate to heart level and notify the provider immediately. For chemotherapy, I would monitor for neutropenia with strict hand hygiene, screening visitors and checking temperature, thrombocytopenia with bleeding precautions such as a soft toothbrush and electric razor and avoiding unnecessary injections, and anemia with rest periods. Any fever is reported immediately because neutropenic fever can progress to sepsis, which shows rapid heart and breathing rates, confusion and low blood pressure. I would escalate right away for compartment syndrome signs, fever, bleeding or signs of sepsis.'},sources:[RULES,NCI_SIDE,CDC_SEPSIS]};
